# Frozen HKO authoring trial, 2026-09-07

This is the ordinary evidence home for the first HKO authoring comparison
described in `memories/authoring-workflow.md`.  The nine artifact files were
copied byte-for-byte from former local branch
`archive/hko-authoring-trial-20260907` at
`7872093e483fb9f0438d4ccb39356a8b0a3bbff1`; this README consolidates and
clarifies that branch's provenance and review limits.  The branch was removed
after the copy and hashes were verified, so the evidence no longer depends on
a hidden or searchable branch ref.

This is frozen calibration evidence, not a maintained workflow or proposed
thesis revision.  The three top-level candidate Markdown files preserve the
compared explanations.  `revised-preview/` is the exact second human-reading
input plus its generated TeX and provenance record.  `repaired-preview/` is a
later unrequested PDF/source snapshot.  The distinctions and review limits are
recorded below; none of these artifacts is an approved current thesis source.

Source baseline: `a3f06cb4`. The unchanged source worktree was
`/tmp/msc-math-unlimited-20260907.IvqpRR/authoring`.

The authoring owner reports that `hko-mechanism-candidate.md` was delivered to
Jörn for formative annotations. Its SHA-256 is
`801062444375df46f7679eb8ab417a58a1759c897a9cca8c516356d6093cce7f`.
This archive does not establish whether or how much he read. Human annotations
had not returned when this snapshot was prepared.

`hko-questions-candidate.md` and `hko-obstruction-candidate.md` are the original
parallel pair: reader questions versus proof obstructions as organizing prompts.
The third, delivered candidate uses a one-variable upper-envelope mechanism
before the geometric bookkeeping. The authoring owner reports that the first
pair produced nearly the same causal structure, whereas the third made a
materially different presentation. These are case observations, not controlled
estimates of prompt effects. Exact prompts and agent execution remain in the
Codex sessions; this directory preserves the compared outputs rather than a
duplicate transcript.

The shortened temporary `hko-for-jorn.md` was not the delivered input and is
not archived as such. No user-imposed word limit was established.

## Review evidence relayed by the authoring owner

The semantic reviewer compared the active chapter 07 sources and chapter 06
criterion, not an independent proof audit. Its exact return for the third
candidate, relayed by `sprint_authoring_loop`:

> One mathematical precision correction in the third candidate: Line47: 'each upper function comes from a smoothly varying feasible action …, divided by the appropriate volume normalization.' Problem: read literally, this omits squaring the action. An action divided by volume has the wrong scaling and is not the stated systolic upper bound. Correction: 'For a smooth feasible action A_i(a), the upper function is U_i(a)=A_i(a)^2/(2 vol_4 K(a)). Feasibility gives c_EHZ(K(a))<=A_i(a).' No material errors found in the toy inequality or its higher-dimensional generalization.

The preserved third candidate contains the correction, applied before delivery.
The full pre-correction candidate is not retained here; the quoted offending
sentence is the available evidence for that defect.

The original pair was reported source-consistent by the semantic review.
The blind reader was a no-history agent given the drafts, not the source or
workflow notes, and used a mathematics-graduate/basic-background reader frame.
Its exact excerpt, relayed by the authoring owner:

> Mechanism makes one real explanatory improvement: the passage f(x)<=s0+x, f(x)<=s0-x => f(x)<=s0-|x| lets me see why several individually increasing/decreasing upper bounds can collectively force a maximum. In the previous pair I had to reconstruct that relationship from the abstract derivative argument. Here I understand, before encountering covectors, why the bound may change with direction and why none must remain sharp.

> The tradeoff is geometric specificity. Mechanism never constructs the HKO body, omits the polar-coordinate inequality defining K(a), and replaces the explicit action-to-ratio formula with 'divided by the appropriate volume normalization.'

The HKO definition and squared-action formula were added before delivery. The
other context tradeoff was not thereby demonstrated resolved. Agent reader
judgments are not evidence of human comprehension or final-gate readiness.

## Later chapter-context finding (separate from the frozen human input)

The authoring owner relayed `chapter_context_review`'s finding: chapter 06
already contained the finite-criterion proof at lines 358–409. The integrated
candidate `4163f3ea`, chapter 07 lines 106–126, repeated it without citation;
the following exact-certificate section, lines 49–70, repeated it again.
The reviewer recommended retaining the toy example, citing the prior lemma,
and compressing the repeated compactness/Taylor argument. This is a parent
summary of the review, not its verbatim return or an independent check here.

After repair `5e206511`, the context worker's exact admission, relayed by the
authoring owner, was:

> The review exposed a real retrieval miss: my initial chapter-06 reading covered its opening and feasible-section lemma, but not the already-present finite-upper-function criterion near its end.

The frozen delivered input above was not changed by this later integration
review or repair. The admission establishes what this worker reports missing,
not a general rule that reading every file is necessary.

## Revised two-page PDF delivered for a second reading

The sprint root reported that Jörn was reading
`revised-preview/hko-revised-preview.pdf`; feedback was pending at archival
time. This is a new input, not a replacement for the first Markdown candidate.
The PDF's SHA-256 is
`759d8f6357e5694edf1401434c5ee6bedcc28cf12e13326a4da89fb7ad0cf536`.

The accompanying generated TeX and provenance JSON are preserved byte-for-byte.
The JSON identifies source revision `a2e613569926450d0bbd72b1e5920dd0c23da5b2`,
external-reference auxiliary baseline `ceffb6b5`, selected source-file hashes,
excerpt omissions and formatting. External reference labels and the native
figure are embedded in the generated TeX; no auxiliary or log directory was
copied. The PDF is the exact human input; the retained TeX and source pointers
support inspecting its construction, not a claim of byte-identical rebuilding.
The human's temporary file was neither edited nor replaced.

## Repaired preview, not requested for human review

`repaired-preview/` preserves the next PDF, generated TeX and Markdown exactly.
Its PDF SHA-256 is
`a62f84dd8de88b0e12e6d9ce2c23fa1ccf49910b4b5ab822d0ef6ebab689ce45`.
The authoring owner reports three pages, 11pt type and 25mm margins, fixed
figure placement, restored first-candidate orientation, and added explicit
construction and smooth-volume bridge. No human-review request was made.
A fresh PDF-only reader was still running at archival time; this snapshot
makes no claim about its result. The original and withdrawn inputs remain
unchanged in their separate directories.
